<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    Consent's cross-section surface, carved into its own leaf project because most of its
    consumers are still in Base: Humans.Application's OnboardingService and
    OnboardingWidgetState read IConsentServiceRead, AccountMergeService evicts through
    IConsentCacheInvalidator, and Humans.Infrastructure's HumansMetricsService reads
    ILegalDocumentSyncServiceRead. A Contracts/ *folder* inside Humans.Consent would
    therefore make Base reference a section and cycle (design §15.5b,
    memory/architecture/section-project-cycle-fix.md).

    Two names left that census on their jobs' way home to this section
    (nobodies-collective/Humans#866): ILegalDocumentSyncRunner's only consumer,
    SyncLegalDocumentsJob, moved at lane 5b-4, and SendReConsentReminderJob — the second
    ILegalDocumentSyncServiceRead reader — moved at lane 5b-5. Both interfaces could follow
    their jobs inward; that is a later pass, and shrinking interface surface needs Peter.

    ConsentReviewFormViewModel is here for the reason step 3b's "move the markup" case
    does not apply: the onboarding widget's Consents step is Onboarding's view over
    Consent's form, so Shell constructs the model for the section's
    _ConsentReviewBody partial. It names no ASP.NET type.

    Everything with no consumer outside the section stays internal: the admin
    legal-document surface, the GitHub connector, the sync service's write half, the
    consent dashboard, both repositories and all three entities.

    References Humans.Interfaces (IApplicationService, IInvalidator) and NodaTime
    (Instant on the snapshots). Both sit below Humans.Application, so the graph stays
    acyclic.
  -->

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Humans.Interfaces\Humans.Interfaces.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="NodaTime" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
